Let $D=[0,2]\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?

I. $$
\displaystyle f(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}x+y,\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

II. $$
\displaystyle g(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}-xy,\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

III. $$
\displaystyle h(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{1}{4}xy,\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

Let $D=[0,2]\times [1,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?

I. $$
\displaystyle f(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{y}{3},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

II. $$
\displaystyle g(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{x}{2},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

III. $$
\displaystyle h(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{x-y}{6},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

Let $D=[1,e]\times [1,e].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?

I. $$
\displaystyle f(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}4xy,\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

II. $$
\displaystyle g(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{2x-y}{4e},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

III. $$
\displaystyle h(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{1}{xy},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

Let $D=[1,2]\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?

I. $$
\displaystyle f(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}2-xy,\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

II. $$
\displaystyle g(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{2x+y}{8},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

III. $$
\displaystyle h(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{x+2y}{8},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

Let $D=[0,1]\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?

I. $$
\displaystyle f(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{2-xy}{3},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

II. $$
\displaystyle g(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{x-y}{4},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$

III. $$
\displaystyle h(x,y)=\{\begin{aligned}\frac{1}{2},\, & (x,y)\in D \\ 0, & \text{otherwise}\end{aligned}
$$
